-- Rendering a `JavaValue.Value` as the text a real `java` run would print, and
-- the one-line `observed` entry point that turns a λ□ program into that text.
--
-- WHY A SEPARATE, TRACKED MODULE. These definitions were written inside
-- `ProbeCorpus.ard`, which imports `Imported.*` -- generated, gitignored output
-- of `test/tools/ast-to-arend`, absent from a fresh checkout. So the only
-- transcription of `Rt.Data.render` in the tree lived in a module that does not
-- typecheck when cloned. Nothing here depends on `Imported.*`, so this module
-- does, and `ProbeCorpus.ard` is left holding only the corpus wiring and the
-- assertions.
--
-- WHAT `renderJ` IS. A transcription of `Rt.Data.render` (`Rt.java:44-62`), which
-- is its own oracle: the printed form of a program's result is what `run.py`
-- records and compares, so reproducing it exactly is what makes this semantics
-- differential-testable against `javac` + `java` at all.
--
--   * a NULLARY constructor prints as just its tag -- `Rt.java:46` returns early
--     on `fields.length == 0`;
--   * otherwise `tag(f1,f2)`, recursively;
--   * `String.valueOf` on a `Long` is the bare number.
--
-- LAYOUT IS DELIBERATELY OMITTED. `Rt.Data.render` indents by nesting depth;
-- `test/run.py`'s `normalize` DELETES all whitespace before comparing (its
-- docstring explains why: the four backends do not share a layout). Reproducing
-- the indentation would be work that the comparison then throws away.
--
-- `Rt.BOX` IS NOT REPRODUCIBLE, and this is a real limit, not an oversight.
-- `Rt.BOX` is `new Object()` (`Rt.java`), so `Rt.runMain`'s
-- `System.out.println(body.apply(BOX))` prints `java.lang.Object@1b6d3586` -- a
-- per-run identity hashcode. No model can produce that. It renders as "BOX"
-- here, and any program whose output contains one is simply not comparable this
-- way; the plan's fallback for a harness row is to normalize the hashcode on
-- both sides, but for an `idp` assertion the program is just excluded.

\import JavaValue
\import JavaEval
\import LambdaBox
\import ToJava
-- `observed` is specifically the `targetLong` observable: `compileClass` is
-- instantiated at `targetLong` (`ToJava.ard`'s default) and run under the
-- matching `rtLong`. That pairing is the point -- it is the configuration
-- `test/run.py`'s recorded java output was produced by.
\import RtLong
\import StringUtil
\import Data.Bool
\import Data.Maybe
\import Data.String

-- A short diagnostic form: says WHICH shape a value has without descending into
-- it. For probing, not for comparison -- `renderJ` is the comparable one.
\func rr (v : Value) : String => \case v \with {
  | vBox => "BOX" | vData tg _ => "Data(" ++ natToStr tg ++ ")"
  | vLong i => "long " ++ intToStr i | vClos _ _ _ _ => "closure"
  | vBuiltin nm _ => "builtin " ++ nm | vNull => "null" | vUninit => "UNINIT"
  | vArr l => "arr@" ++ natToStr l | vStr s => "str " ++ s
  | vExcObj c m => "EXC " ++ c ++ ": " ++ m
  | _ => "other"
}

-- `d` bounds the nesting depth. A `Data`'s fields live in the store, so this
-- follows `Loc`s and cannot be structural on the value; the depth bound is what
-- makes it total. "..." marks a truncation, so a too-small `d` can never be
-- mistaken for agreement.
\func renderJ (d : Nat) (st : Store) (v : Value) : String \elim d
  | 0 => "..."
  | suc d => \case v \with {
    | vLong i => intToStr i
    | vInt k => natToStr k
    | vStr t => t
    | vBox => "BOX"
    | vData tg loc => \case storeGet st loc \with {
      | nothing => "?nostore"
      -- Tested on the length rather than by matching the array, which Arend
      -- rejects inside this nested \case.
      | just cells => \case natEq cells.len 0 \with {
        | true => natToStr tg
        | false => natToStr tg ++ "(" ++ renderFields d st cells ++ ")"
      }
    }
    | vClos _ _ _ _ => "closure"
    | vBuiltin nm _ => "builtin " ++ nm
    | vNull => "null"
    | vUninit => "UNINIT"
    | vArr l => "arr@" ++ natToStr l
    | vExcObj c m => "EXC " ++ c ++ ": " ++ m
    | _ => "other"
  }

\func renderFields (d : Nat) (st : Store) (cells : Array Value) : String \elim cells
  | nil => ""
  | c :: nil => renderJ d st c
  | c :: (c2 :: cs) => renderJ d st c ++ "," ++ renderFields d st (c2 :: cs)

-- The observable of a compiled program, as text, comparable with the value
-- `run.py` recorded from javac + java.
--
-- `__main` is the entry point `ToJava.compileClass` emits, and `jExprStmt` is
-- emitted only for `Rt.runMain` (`ToJava.ard:386`) -- never
-- `System.out.println` -- so a program's whole observable IS the value `__main`
-- returns, and no output stream needs modelling. That was checked, not assumed.
--
-- The three non-value outcomes are named rather than collapsed, because the
-- difference is exactly what a differential test needs: "STUCK" is a real
-- disagreement with `java`, "OUT OF FUEL" is a budget that was too small.
\func observed (p : LBProgram) (n : Nat) : String =>
  \case callStatic rtLong (compileClass {targetLong} p) n nil "__main" nil \with {
    | rOutOfFuel => "OUT OF FUEL"
    | rStuck => "STUCK"
    | rOk q => \case q.2 \with {
      | eVal v => renderJ 40 q.1 v
      | eExc v => "THROWN"
    }
  }

-- The short form of the same, for probing an unfamiliar program.
\func go (p : LBProgram) (n : Nat) : String =>
  \case callStatic rtLong (compileClass {targetLong} p) n nil "__main" nil \with {
    | rOutOfFuel => "OUT OF FUEL"
    | rStuck => "STUCK"
    | rOk q => \case q.2 \with {
      | eVal v => "VALUE " ++ rr v
      | eExc v => "THROWN " ++ rr v
    }
  }
